# tests/mpx4-tcp's run list (px23). kmain_sockets runs it from the
# initramfs as /etc/pax-run with tools/net-peer's TCP script at the
# other end of a socket netdev; tools/linux-net runs it on Linux with
# the same script at the other end of a tap device; the two outputs and
# exit statuses are compared byte for byte. One command a line, words separated by single spaces, the first the
# program's path. In order: what stream sockets answer with nothing on
# the wire; a forking server and its client over loopback; a wolf
# program serving itself; a transfer over loopback; connections opened
# and closed over loopback; a listen queue nobody empties; the echo
# server, asked by the script and then soaked; the forking echo server;
# the client; a transfer out, a writer that closes with bytes unsent,
# a transfer in and one into a receiver that reads nothing at first;
# connections out; how a
# connection ends (refused, unanswered, reset, timed out, an ICMP
# error for its SYN: the port's, the host's); the scripted
# faults with PAX the client (the port names the fault) and with PAX
# the server; the loss matrix out and in (the port's last two digits
# name the rates); a wolf program serving another machine.
/bin/tcperr
/bin/tcploop
/bin/tcpwolf
/bin/tcpbulk loop 16777216
/bin/tcpsoak loop 10000
/bin/tcpfull
/bin/tcpecho 7 10.0.2.2 9099
/bin/tcpecho 7 10.0.2.2 9098 fork
/bin/tcpclient 10.0.2.2 9099
/bin/tcpbulk send 10.0.2.2 9100 16777216
/bin/tcpbulk leave 10.0.2.2 9110 60000
/bin/tcpbulk recv 7100 10.0.2.2 9097
/bin/tcpbulk recv 7140 10.0.2.2 9097 nap 1500
/bin/tcpsoak to 10.0.2.2 9099 10000
/bin/tcpend 10.0.2.2 9
/bin/tcpend 10.0.2.2 9001 syncnt 1
/bin/tcpend 10.0.2.2 9301
/bin/tcpend 10.0.2.2 9302 timeout 3000
/bin/tcpend 10.0.2.2 9003
/bin/tcpend 10.0.2.2 9004 syncnt 1
/bin/tcpclient 10.0.2.2 9201
/bin/tcpclient 10.0.2.2 9202
/bin/tcpclient 10.0.2.2 9203
